<InsightArticle title="The context window is bigger than the document space you can plan around" description="A frozen AIRH context record becomes a document budget only after explicit reserves. A worked guide to capacity, counting and the quality checks subtraction cannot replace." date="2026-11-14" type="Explainer" readTime="9 min" snapshot="2026-11-14" standfirst="AIRH's frozen Nemotron 3.5 Lightning record lists a 262,144-token context window. In a teaching budget with 12,000 tokens of fixed input and 32,000 reserved for output, the document allowance is 218,144 tokens.">
<p>Those three numbers describe different things. The first is a capacity recorded in our catalogue. The second is an invented allowance for material outside the document. The third is what subtraction leaves for the document under that plan. Treating the first as the document allowance would spend space that the example has already assigned elsewhere.</p>
<p>This guide uses the 14 November 2026 frozen AIRH snapshot, inspected on that date. The chosen record makes the arithmetic concrete; it does not establish the service conditions of a particular endpoint. The reserves are assumptions, not advertised output limits, measured prompt sizes or a recommendation to use this model. No document was submitted and no model quality was tested.</p>
<h2>The missing space already has a job</h2>
<p>A context window is the capacity available to the material considered within a request under the service's accounting rules. The exact categories and limits need checking against the intended service. For this worked plan, input and the reserved generated response share the stated capacity. Fixed input means everything we have chosen to include apart from the document, held constant across the rows.</p>
<p>That fixed input could include instructions and a question in a real application. Here it is just a labelled 12,000-token assumption. We do not claim that an application needs that amount, or that it includes a measured service overhead. Separating it gives the planner a place to put counted material rather than pretending the whole window belongs to one attachment.</p>
<WorkedTable headers={['Recorded window','Assumed fixed input','Assumed output reserve','Remaining document tokens']} rows={d.documentSpace.map(r=>[r.window,r.fixedInput,r.output,r.document])} caption="Capacity arithmetic on one frozen AIRH record. Reserves are hypothetical and not claims about supported maximum outputs. Remaining document space = recorded window minus fixed input minus output reserve. Actual service accounting needs verification." />
<p>The middle row leaves 218,144 tokens. A smaller output reserve leaves 242,144; a larger one leaves 186,144. None changes the catalogue window. They represent different allocations within one teaching budget. An allowance is a planning choice about how much space to leave, not a measurement of how much a response will actually consume.</p>
<p>This is why a document that falls below the headline window can still exceed the application's planned budget. The relevant comparison is the complete request and its required reserve, rather than the attachment alone. A larger allowance for the answer reduces the space available for the input in this example, even if the document has not changed.</p>
<h2>Why a word count cannot fill this table</h2>
<p>The table uses tokens throughout. Tokens are the units a model's text processing counts, and a tokeniser is the procedure that produces them from text. A word count is a different measure. This guide deliberately supplies no universal conversion between the two. A token count needs to correspond to the intended model and to the actual material that will be sent.</p>
<p>A practical preparation record could retain the document version, the counting procedure and the resulting count. If the document is converted before submission, the counted version needs to be that converted text. Counting one version and sending another breaks the link between the preparation record and the request, even when both are casually called the same document.</p>
<p>Tables, extracted page headers and repeated material deserve attention during that conversion. The proposal here is to inspect what the application sends, without assuming any particular extraction tool's behaviour. An application may produce a request very different from the visible file. The counted request is the object whose size matters to this budget.</p>
<p>Attachments and text-only prompts can also follow different accounting paths in a product. Our catalogue row cannot settle those differences. Before using the arithmetic operationally, the endpoint's documentation and a controlled request need to establish which capacity is applicable and which reported usage corresponds to the submitted material.</p>
<h2>What happens when the document is too large?</h2>
<p>A preparation stage can compare the counted input with the chosen allowance before sending it. If the document exceeds that allowance, the workflow needs a named outcome rather than silently losing material. That outcome might be an explicit refusal to proceed or a separate method for selecting evidence. Neither is implemented or evaluated by this article.</p>
<p>Silent truncation would change the task's evidence. Truncation means cutting material off. A question about a detail near the missing end would then be asked of a different document from the one the user supplied. A useful trial records whether anything was omitted and why, so a later incorrect answer can be investigated against the text actually available.</p>
<p>A summary can reduce size, but it also creates another representation to check. The relevant question is whether it preserves the facts required for the intended task. Subtraction cannot answer that. A summary that fits is a capacity success; whether the resulting answer is faithful remains an evaluation question with its own evidence.</p>
<p>Likewise, selecting passages can make an input smaller while excluding the necessary passage. The smaller request should not be called equivalent merely because the user asks the same question. Our earlier retrieval guide considers that distinction. This guide's contribution is narrower: the capacity plan should expose the point where a different treatment of the document became necessary.</p>
<h2>Space is a limit, not an evidence score</h2>
<p>Successful submission would establish that a request was accepted under that service's rules. It would not establish that the model used each passage well. No quality result is implied by the 218,144-token allowance. A trial could include questions whose answers lie in different parts of a document, with references to the relevant passages and a written pass rule.</p>
<p>The pass rule defines what counts as an acceptable answer. A response may need to identify the right passage, state its content accurately and avoid unsupported additions. Those requirements should be assessed directly. A large window is useful context for planning the trial, but it cannot replace checking the answers produced in it.</p>
<p>The output reserve also needs its own interpretation. If it is too small for a complete answer, the application may need a different plan. If it is generous, the unused allowance does not itself prove waste. It is capacity held aside under the stated method, and the observed charged usage belongs in a separate cost record.</p>
<p>Our <a href="/transparency/#practitioner-planning">transparency receipt</a> preserves the selected catalogue record, the source file's hash and each subtraction. The snapshot's listed date is not a new verification of the endpoint today. Actual output limits, product handling of attachments and any additional charges remain outside this teaching calculation.</p>
<p>The recorded 262,144-token figure was selected from the snapshot's below-million group, not calculated as an average across the catalogue. That distinction prevents the example becoming a claim about what a typical model accepts. A different row would give a different starting capacity, while the accounting method would still require the planner to state the material and reserves included.</p>
<p>A revision to the document or instructions can invalidate the earlier fit decision. The new count belongs beside the new version, with the earlier one retained for comparison. This is especially useful when someone asks why yesterday's request fitted but today's does not. The answer may be a changed input or reserve rather than a changed provider limit, and a versioned preparation record can distinguish those possibilities without guessing.</p>
<p>A useful planning result is therefore conditional: this document count fits this allowance under these stated accounting assumptions. It can travel with the prompt and document versions into a trial. That is a more useful statement than saying the file fits a large model, because a reviewer can see what was counted and what space was deliberately left unused.</p>
<p>Where the service reports counts that differ from the preparation estimate, the discrepancy deserves an explanation before more documents are sent. The estimate and the service report may cover different material. Keeping both makes that difference inspectable, while overwriting the estimate with the later number would hide the reason the original capacity decision was made.</p>
</InsightArticle>
